From: A50 port <noreply@example.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fimc-is: release the group worker's semaphores on close, unconditionally

Closing a fimc V4L2 node could hang the closing process forever in an
unkillable D-state, taking the PM freezer down with it:

  gst-plugin-scan  D   __switch_to __schedule schedule_timeout
                        wait_for_completion kthread_stop
                        fimc_is_group_close fimc_is_ischain_isp_close
                        fimc_is_ixs_video_close v4l2_release ____fput

Captured end to end in the fsynced kmsg log (a50-ubuntu-touch experiment
018, crash D). SIGKILL cannot move a D-state task, so even killing the
scanner left the boot one PM cycle away from an aborted suspend.

The group worker (kthread_worker_fn on gtask->task) runs shot works.
fimc_is_group_shot() downs gtask->smp_resource - and for sync shots
group->smp_trigger - only AFTER passing its first REQUEST_STOP check.
fimc_is_group_task_stop() released the resource semaphore only when the
semaphore's wait list was already non-empty:

    if (!list_empty(&gtask->smp_resource.wait_list))
            up(&gtask->smp_resource);
    kthread_stop(gtask->task);

That is a race. A stop that runs between the worker's REQUEST_STOP check
and its down() sees an empty wait list, skips the up(), and the worker
sleeps on a semaphore whose only producers (frame completions) are gone -
the client that streamed the frames has closed and the sensor is stopped.
kthread_stop() then waits for a thread that can never leave work->func,
while the V4L2 closer sleeps in the kernel holding the node.

Fix: up() both semaphores unconditionally on the close path. A spurious
up() is harmless by construction - the worker's next REQUEST_STOP check
(after each down) bails to p_err_ignore, which releases exactly what it
took, and both semaphores are re-initialised by the next group/gtask
start (sema_init in fimc_is_group_init / fimc_is_group_task_start), so
no count outlives the teardown. The trigger up() lives in
fimc_is_group_close() rather than inside task_stop() so the
streaming-stop caller is untouched.

No public fix exists to lift: the newest trees carrying this driver
(LineageOS android_kernel_samsung_exynos9820, lineage-23.2) still have
the conditional up().

This is Samsung vendor code; fimc-is2 is not in mainline, so there is no
upstream to send this to.
--- a/drivers/media/platform/exynos/fimc-is2/fimc-is-groupmgr.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/exynos/fimc-is2/fimc-is-groupmgr.c
@@ -1049,11 +1049,17 @@
 	set_bit(FIMC_IS_GTASK_REQUEST_STOP, &gtask->state);
 
 
+	/* The up() below must not depend on the wait list being non-empty:
+	 * the worker downs smp_resource only after passing its first
+	 * REQUEST_STOP check, so a stop racing that window sees an empty
+	 * list, skips the release, and kthread_stop() waits forever on a
+	 * down() that nothing will complete again.
+	 */
 	if (!list_empty(&gtask->smp_resource.wait_list)) {
 		warn("gtask(%d) is not empty", gtask->id);
 		fimc_is_kernel_log_dump(false);
-		up(&gtask->smp_resource);
 	}
+	up(&gtask->smp_resource);
 
 	/*
 	 * flush kthread wait until all work is complete
@@ -1836,6 +1842,14 @@
 		return -EMFILE;
 	}
 
+	/* Sync-shot works block on the closing group's trigger semaphore
+	 * (fimc_is_group_shot: down_interruptible(&group->smp_trigger));
+	 * release them before stopping the worker, or kthread_stop() can
+	 * wait forever. Harmless when nobody waits: group_init re-inits
+	 * the semaphore and the REQUEST_STOP checks make the worker bail.
+	 */
+	up(&group->smp_trigger);
+
 	ret = fimc_is_group_task_stop(groupmgr, gtask, slot);
 	if (ret)
 		mgerr("fimc_is_group_task_stop is fail(%d)", group, group, ret);
